<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  THE TRACK DETAIL DOCK MUST NOT CRUSH ITS CONTENTS (fix/detail-pane-layout).

  Reported from a screenshot: in a dock about 500 px tall the Feel Flow
  header sat ON the Analyze / Learn buttons, "compliance:" sat on
  "counter-tension:", and the FX strip's "+ Add Effect" button was drawn over
  the plugin list. Minimum heights, in the reporter's words, "appear not to be
  respected".

  THEY WERE NOT, AND THE MECHANISM IS THE POINT. Qt's qSmartMinSize() REPLACES
  a widget's layout-derived minimum with an explicit minimumSize() when one is
  set — it does not take the larger of the two. STrackDetailPanel set a 100 px
  floor on its content widget and SPluginEffectStrip set another on itself, so
  a section that genuinely needs ~450 px told the dock it fitted in 100, and a
  QBoxLayout handed less space than its minimum does not refuse: it distributes
  the shortfall and the children end up drawn on top of one another. The fix is
  an honest minimum plus a QScrollArea — the shape SClipPropertiesPanel has
  always had — so a short dock SCROLLS instead of compressing.

  WHY A GEOMETRY GATE AND NOT A SCREENSHOT. `screenshot` grabs the SCREEN's
  root window, blank under QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen, and proves nothing about
  one dock's paint (main/testkit/CONTRACT.md's standing note). Crushing is a
  GEOMETRY defect, and geometry is exactly what a headless run can measure:
  assert-track-detail-layout builds the REAL panel — the real FX strip, the
  real Feel Flow section, bound to the real track — off screen at a given size
  and counts (a) widgets given less height than they report needing and
  (b) pairs of widgets in one layout tree whose rectangles intersect.

  WATCHED FAILING on the pre-fix binary, which is what makes the numbers below
  mean anything:

    height 200:  crushed=13  overlap=7  scrollNeeded=0  worst=QWidget(200<447)
    height 260:  crushed=13  overlap=7  scrollNeeded=0  worst=QWidget(260<447)
    height 500:  crushed=0   overlap=0                      (tall enough: fine)
    height 900:  crushed=0   overlap=0                      (tall enough: fine)

  i.e. the content widget was handed 200 px for 447 px of content and thirteen
  widgets were squeezed, seven of them into each other — and the panel did not
  scroll, because nothing could scroll. Post-fix every height reads 0 / 0, and
  the two short ones report scrollNeeded=1 instead.

  NOT GATED: what any of this LOOKS like (a geometry relation, not a palette),
  the dock's own resize/float/close round trip through Qt's opaque
  ui/windowState blob, and the Clip Detail pane's layout — it already had the
  scroll area this fix gives the Track Detail pane, and no verb builds it for
  measurement.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="track_detail_layout">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_sawtooth.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- A plugin row, so the FX strip has real content to be crushed. The
         Feel Flow section below it is unconditional — every track has one. -->
    <insert-plugin trackPath="0" slotIndex="0"
                   format="clap" uid="tw.test.clap.gain"
                   name="Smaragd Test Gain" vendor="Smaragd"
                   path="twtestclap.clap" nIn="2" nOut="2"/>

    <!-- ============ 1. a dock far too short for the content ============ -->
    <!-- The failing configuration. Nothing may be compressed and nothing may
         overlap; the overflow becomes a scrollbar instead. -->
    <assert-track-detail-layout trackPath="0" panelWidth="320" panelHeight="200"
                                maxCrushed="0" maxOverlap="0"
                                contains="scrollNeeded=1"/>

    <!-- The height the reported screenshot's dock was near. -->
    <assert-track-detail-layout trackPath="0" panelWidth="320" panelHeight="260"
                                maxCrushed="0" maxOverlap="0"
                                contains="scrollNeeded=1"/>

    <!-- ============ 2. a dock with room needs no scrollbar ============= -->
    <!-- The other half of the statement: the scroll area is not a permanent
         fixture that steals space, it engages only when the content does not
         fit. A fix that simply always scrolled would pass part 1 and fail
         here. -->
    <assert-track-detail-layout trackPath="0" panelWidth="320" panelHeight="900"
                                maxCrushed="0" maxOverlap="0"
                                contains="scrollNeeded=0"/>

    <!-- ============ 3. a NARROW dock, at the reported height =========== -->
    <!-- Width matters too: a narrower panel wraps labels and pushes rows
         taller, which is what turns a marginal height into a crushed one. -->
    <assert-track-detail-layout trackPath="0" panelWidth="240" panelHeight="260"
                                maxCrushed="0" maxOverlap="0"/>

    <!-- ============ 4. a SECOND plugin: more content, same rule ======== -->
    <insert-plugin trackPath="0" slotIndex="1"
                   format="clap" uid="tw.test.clap.stereoskew"
                   name="Smaragd Test Stereo Skew" vendor="Smaragd"
                   path="twtestclap.clap" nIn="2" nOut="2"/>
    <assert-plugin-strip trackIndex="0" slotCount="2"/>
    <assert-track-detail-layout trackPath="0" panelWidth="320" panelHeight="260"
                                maxCrushed="0" maxOverlap="0"
                                contains="scrollNeeded=1"/>
    <!-- ...and with two rows, even a tall dock now overflows. -->
    <assert-track-detail-layout trackPath="0" panelWidth="320" panelHeight="380"
                                maxCrushed="0" maxOverlap="0"/>
  </actions>
</SActionScript>
